// LEGAL POSITIONING NOTE — PUERTO RICO IS THE MOST PERMISSIVE
// FRAMEWORK IN OUR ENTIRE 87-COUNTRY COVERAGE. THIS CHANGES THE
// OFFER COMPLETELY. READ ALL:
// - There is NO Puerto Rico law expressly regulating home education,
//   and none establishing criteria for how a home school operates.
// - CONSTITUTIONAL BASE: ARTICLE II, SECTION 5 of the Constitution
//   of Puerto Rico is cited as protecting the right to educate at
//   home. Commentary also cites the FIRST AMENDMENT (religious
//   liberty) and the FOURTEENTH AMENDMENT Due Process Clause
//   (fundamental parental right to direct the care, custody and
//   control of children) of the US Constitution.
// - COMPULSORY ATTENDANCE: education is compulsory from age five,
//   3 LPRA sec. 391. Reported in practice as roughly ages 5 to 18.
//   After 16, the law no longer requires a specific number of school
//   days. Confirm current detail.
// - *** THE MECHANISM — L.P.R.A. 18 SECTION 2322 ***: the statute
//   uses the English word "homeschooling". In a home-education
//   programme, the PARENT is the "NON-GOVERNMENTAL ENTITY" under
//   whose auspices the school operates, AND is also the SCHOOL
//   DIRECTOR (L.P.R.A. 18 sec. 2; PR Constitution Art. II sec. 5).
//   Home educators are therefore exempt from compulsory PUBLIC
//   school attendance because they operate a non-governmental school.
// - NO REGISTRATION, NO NOTIFICATION, NO APPROVAL: home schools are
//   NOT required to seek approval or recognition from any government
//   agency, nor to notify their educational programme. There is NO
//   official registry of homeschoolers in Puerto Rico.
// - NO TEACHER CERTIFICATION required; no education degree required.
// - NO MANDATORY STATE CURRICULUM and no routine government
//   supervision of homeschooling families.
// - "ADEQUATE" EDUCATION is not defined in law; it is understood as
//   one that adequately prepares the child for adult life and its
//   responsibilities, taking into account the child's individual
//   characteristics.
// - WITHDRAWAL: only required if the child was already enrolled. It
//   is strongly recommended to give the principal a written letter.
//   Public schools use a withdrawal form with the home-education
//   code "V14". Request copies of the academic record — transcript,
//   test results, immunisation certification.
// - NO PUERTO RICO CASE LAW on home education is reported.
// - ADSEF (Departamento de la Familia) is reported to recognise home
//   education as meeting academic requirements for its purposes.
// - RESOURCE ORGANISATIONS: T'CHERs — The Caribbean Center of Home
//   Education Resources (tchers.net) — is an island-wide orientation
//   and resource body offering free guidance; it is NOT a school.
//   HSLDA publishes Puerto Rico material in Spanish. Mention both
//   generously; they help families and are not competitors.
// - *** THE COMMERCIAL INSIGHT — THIS IS THE WHOLE PITCH ***: MOST
//   HOME EDUCATORS IN PUERTO RICO OPERATE AS NON-GOVERNMENT SCHOOLS
//   AND USE THEIR OWN TRANSCRIPTS to evidence grades and completed
//   work, RATHER THAN AN OFFICIAL DIPLOMA. That freedom is real and
//   valuable — and it creates one specific weakness at university
//   admission: a PARENT-ISSUED TRANSCRIPT IS SELF-ATTESTED.
//   Cambridge IGCSE, A-Level, IB and AP are EXTERNALLY EXAMINED by
//   an independent board. THAT is what we add, and it is the single
//   most useful thing we can offer a Puerto Rican homeschooling
//   family. Lead with it. Never disparage self-issued transcripts —
//   they are lawful and widely used — simply explain what external
//   examination adds on top.
// - CONSEQUENCE: FULL-TIME IS GENUINELY AVAILABLE HERE, more freely
//   than in Chile (which needs exámenes libres) and far more than
//   anywhere else in our coverage. Offer it openly, alongside the
//   supplementary option.
// - Smartious is not a Puerto Rico Department of Education licensed
//   school and does not issue a Puerto Rico diploma. Say so.
// TIMEZONE: AST (UTC-4), no daylight saving — SEVEN HOURS behind
// Nairobi. Puerto Rican mornings and very early afternoons both work.
// NOTE: because PR does not observe DST while the US mainland does,
// the offset to the mainland shifts seasonally — irrelevant to us
// but worth knowing for families coordinating with mainland relatives.
// MARKET NOTE: US territory, USD currency, US federal financial aid
// context, College Board and AP thoroughly established. San Juan
// holds the international and bilingual tier (Baldwin School,
// Robinson School, Saint John's, Academia María Reina, Commonwealth-
// Parkville). Dorado and the north coast have drawn substantial
// mainland relocation under Puerto Rico's tax incentive regime.
// Ponce anchors the south with a medical school. Mayagüez holds the
// UPR engineering campus — one of the strongest engineering
// faculties in the Caribbean. Fajardo, Vieques and Culebra sit east
// with genuine access constraints. Hurricanes María (2017) and
// Fiona (2022) and the 2020 earthquakes caused prolonged school
// disruption and hundreds of public school closures — mention
// FACTUALLY and respectfully as a continuity argument, never
// sensationally.
